-- title:
--   Constructing a two-coordinate coloring array
-- statement:
--   Let $S,B,D$ be the machine's space, word, and call-depth limits. Choose coordinates $a,b<W$ and a map $\phi:\{0,1\}^2\to\{0,1,2\}$. Suppose $n\le2^W$, the destination satisfies $\mathrm{dst}+n\le\mathrm{work}$, and
--   $$\mathrm{work}+W+1\le S\le B,\qquad 2^{W+1}\le B,\qquad d+W+2\le D.$$
--   For a program containing the specified coloring, powers-table, and mask-erasure procedures, the array constructor terminates from any initial memory $\mu$ within $n(200W+310)+17W+125$ steps. Its final memory $\nu$ satisfies
--   $$\nu(\mathrm{dst}+i)=\phi(\operatorname{bit}_a(i),\operatorname{bit}_b(i))\quad(i<n),\qquad \nu(\mathrm{work}+j)=2^j\quad(j\le W).$$
--   Every address below $\mathrm{work}$ outside the destination interval retains its initial value.
--
--   This implements a coloring parameter as a literal array while retaining the powers table used to read its two coordinates.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/LightColorArray.lean#L37-L99

